{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook with a sharp, gripping narrative and impeccable direction by david fincher. based on ben mezrich’s book “the accidental billionaires,” the movie transforms what could have been a dry tech startup story into a compelling drama about ambition, betrayal, friendship, and the cost of innovation.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing the character’s intense intellect, social awkwardness, and simmering resentment with unnerving precision. his portrayal is not a caricature but a nuanced study of a brilliant yet emotionally stunted young man driven by validation and a desire to prove himself. justin timberlake is equally compelling as sean parker, the charismatic, chaotic founder of napster, whose influence on zuckerberg is both intoxicating and destructive. timberlake brings a magnetic energy that perfectly contrasts zuckerberg’s cold demeanor.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is a triumph—rapid-fire, witty, and layered with subtext. every line feels purposeful, every exchange a chess move in a high-stakes game of power and perception. the dialogue crackles with intelligence, and the film’s structure, framed by two legal depositions, adds a thrilling tension that propels the story forward even during quieter moments.\n\ndanny cohen’s cinematography is sleek and cold, mirroring the digital world the characters inhabit, while trent reznor and atticus ross’s haunting, electronic score elevates the film to an almost operatic level. the music doesn’t just accompany the scenes—it defines them, echoing the loneliness and isolation beneath the surface of digital connection.\n\nwhat makes the film so enduring is its ambiguity. it doesn’t clearly label anyone as hero or villain. zuckerberg is both a genius and a jerk; the winklevoss twins are both wronged and entitled; parker is a visionary and a manipulator. the movie refuses easy answers, instead forcing viewers to sit with the uncomfortable truth that greatness often comes at a steep human cost.\n\nthe social network isn’t just about facebook—it’s about how the digital age reshapes identity, relationships, and power. it’s a modern tragedy wrapped in a tech thriller, and it remains one of the most insightful films about the 21st century’s defining innovation. flawless in execution, haunting in theme, and unforgettable in impact.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0004889961564913392}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.31129345297813416}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.47485053539276123}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.001073380233719945}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0032333259005099535}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00011812942830147222}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.1336939334869385}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.005247865337878466}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.6555284261703491}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.756337511935271e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00025293012731708586}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -1.0619215965270996}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -1.4777511358261108}, {"token": " sharp", "logprob": -0.45300740003585815}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.16143549978733063}, {"token": " gripping", "logprob": -2.8274967670440674}, {"token": " narrative", "logprob": -0.07309775799512863}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.5488550066947937}, {"token": " impeccable", "logprob": -1.362821102142334}, {"token": " direction", "logprob": -0.33643773198127747},